        "Obsidian": "# Obsidian\n- A vault is a folder of Markdown files; <found> names the vaults and their top folders with counts, and the daily-notes folder.\n- folder_overview on the vault, then list_note_files with include/exclude (folders relative to the vault) and days, then import_note_files in batches of 300 with root set to the vault. Each file becomes its own note with its title; ![[pictures]] come along, [[links]] become mentions.\n- Leave out by default: the daily-notes folder, folders named Personal, Journal, Private, Templates, Archive, .trash, and anything the person said to leave out.",
        "Notion": "# Notion\n- Notion is reached only through its own reading tools, never a browser: Notion's tools (the MCP server \"notion\": notion-search, notion-fetch). Search for the pages and databases the plan names (or the person's research workspace), fetch each page, and add it with add_note (title = the page's title, markdown = its content, source = the page's link). Without those tools, bring nothing in and say Notion is not connected.\n- Bring in pages, not every database row: a database becomes one note listing its rows unless the plan says otherwise.",
        "Apple Notes": "# Apple Notes\n- apple_notes_folders lists the folders with counts; apple_notes_list lists notes (id, name, folder, modified) filtered by folder, days and words; import_apple_notes brings notes in by id, each as its own note with its pictures.\n- The first call makes macOS ask the person to let Engelbart control Notes. If a tool says permission was refused, call needs_you with kind \"permission\" and reason \"Allow Engelbart to read Apple Notes\".\n- Leave out by default: Recently Deleted, Quick Notes that are a line or two, shopping and to-do lists.",
        "OneNote": "# OneNote\n- Engelbart's browser (browser_open, browser_read, browser_click, browser_type, browser_press, browser_scroll, browser_wait, browser_screenshot, browser_download) is a real Chromium window kept hidden, with the sign-ins the person made in Engelbart. It opens only this app's sites, and it only reads: click links, folders, list items, tabs and buttons that open or show something; type only into the app's search box. Anything that could change the account is refused. browser_read lists what is on the page with a ref for each control (use the ref to click or type) and the page's text; browser_screenshot shows it when the text is not enough.\n- Start at https://www.onenote.com/notebooks (it redirects to OneNote on the web). Open a notebook, then each section and page the plan wants; browser_read a page's text and add_note it (title = the page title, source = its address). If the page asks to sign in (a login form, \"Log in\" button, a redirect to an accounts page), call needs_you with kind \"signin\" and wait: never type a password yourself. After it returns done, reload the start page and go on.\n- A Microsoft sign-in may ask which account: that is the person's choice, so call needs_you.",
        "Google Docs": "# Google Drive and Docs\n- The person knows this app as Google Drive: call it that in anything they read (needs_you reasons, your summary).\n- Engelbart's browser (browser_open, browser_read, browser_click, browser_type, browser_press, browser_scroll, browser_wait, browser_screenshot, browser_download) is a real Chromium window kept hidden, with the sign-ins the person made in Engelbart. It opens only this app's sites, and it only reads: click links, folders, list items, tabs and buttons that open or show something; type only into the app's search box. Anything that could change the account is refused. browser_read lists what is on the page with a ref for each control (use the ref to click or type) and the page's text; browser_screenshot shows it when the text is not enough.\n- To see what is there: browser_open https://drive.google.com/drive/recent (recent files), https://drive.google.com/drive/my-drive (folders), or https://drive.google.com/drive/search?q=<words>. Each file row in browser_read carries its Drive id (id) and its name; the row's label says its kind (Google Docs, Google Sheets, Google Slides, PDF, folder). Open a folder by browser_open https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/<id>.\n- To bring files in: import_google_files with items [{ id, kind, name }], kind doc, sheet, slides or file (a pdf or any uploaded file). Docs become notes (exported as Markdown into the library), Sheets notes of their first sheet as a table, Slides notes of their text, pdfs and other files go into the library. It fetches each file itself, with no prompt. Never use Drive's or Docs' Download or Export menus, and never open files one by one to copy their text.\n- If the page asks to sign in (a login form, \"Log in\" button, a redirect to an accounts page), call needs_you with kind \"signin\" and wait: never type a password yourself. After it returns done, reload the start page and go on. Google refuses some embedded sign-ins; needs_you lets the person sign in or bring their Chrome sign-in over.\n- Leave out by default: files shared with the person that they never opened, forms, and anything older than the plan's time range.",
        "Gmail": "# Gmail\n- Gmail is never brought in whole: it is searched, the person approves threads by name, and only those come in. Use the Gmail tools, not browser_open or browser_read, to look at mail.\n- First gmail_accounts: the accounts signed in to in Engelbart's browser, each with its n (its place in the browser's sign-in order) and address. Never assume n 0. If it says to sign in, call needs_you with kind \"signin\" and reason \"Sign in to Google\".\n- A survey: with one account, search it; with more than one, search each and say which account (by address) each thread is in, and give every account's n, address and ik in \"notes\" (\"account 1 lee@uni.edu, ik cd6f0fe68b\"): the librarian asks the person which one to look in. Build each gmail_search from words in <project> (its topic, methods, names of studies) and the people it names, a few searches of two or three words each, such as \"protocol pilot\" or from:lee; never an empty search or the period alone (gmail_search adds the period itself). Report each thread as an item: id its thread id (thread-f:…), label its subject, kind thread, date, where its people.\n- An import: the plan gives the account's n and ik and the thread ids the person approved. Call import_gmail_threads with exactly those (threads [{ id, subject }], up to 50 a call), nothing else; a thread brought in before is skipped. If the plan has no n and ik and gmail_accounts finds more than one account, do not guess: bring nothing in and say so.\n- Never open threads one by one to copy them, never search for more to bring in than the plan names, and never download attachments: their names are listed in each note.",
        "Evernote": "# Evernote\n- When Evernote's own tools are there (the MCP server \"evernote\": search_notebooks, search_notes, semantic_search, get_note), use them and no browser: search_notebooks for the notebooks, search_notes (Evernote's search syntax, with notebook, tag and date filters) or semantic_search for the notes the plan wants, get_note for each one's content, and add_note it (title = the note's title, markdown = its content, source = \"evernote:<note id>\"). Use only its tools that read; never create, edit, move, tag or delete anything. If they say the sign-in ended, call needs_you with kind \"connector\" and reason \"Connect Evernote\".\n- Without those tools, Engelbart's browser: Engelbart's browser (browser_open, browser_read, browser_click, browser_type, browser_press, browser_scroll, browser_wait, browser_screenshot, browser_download) is a real Chromium window kept hidden, with the sign-ins the person made in Engelbart. It opens only this app's sites, and it only reads: click links, folders, list items, tabs and buttons that open or show something; type only into the app's search box. Anything that could change the account is refused. browser_read lists what is on the page with a ref for each control (use the ref to click or type) and the page's text; browser_screenshot shows it when the text is not enough.\n- Start at https://www.evernote.com/client/web. The Notes list shows titles; open each note the plan wants, browser_read its text and add_note it (title, markdown, source = the note's address). Notebooks are in the sidebar under Notebooks. If the page asks to sign in (a login form, \"Log in\" button, a redirect to an accounts page), call needs_you with kind \"signin\" and wait: never type a password yourself. After it returns done, reload the start page and go on.",
        "Linear": "# Linear\n- Linear is reached only through its own reading tools, never a browser: the Linear MCP server (usually named \"linear\"): list_teams, list_projects, list_issues, list_documents, list_cycles, then get_project, get_issue, get_document, list_comments, get_status_updates for what the plan wants. Use only tools that read.\n- Find the projects (and their issues and documents) that belong with the plan's projects: list_projects, and list_issues filtered by project, team or words from the plan. Bring in a project as one note (title = the project's name, markdown = its description, status, milestones and latest status update, then its issues as a list with their identifiers and states), and a document or a long issue the plan wants as its own note (source = its Linear link).\n- Without those tools, or if they say the sign-in ended, bring nothing in and call needs_you with kind \"connector\" and reason \"Connect Linear\".\n- Leave out by default: closed or cancelled issues older than the plan's time range, and teams that have nothing to do with the plan.",
        "Granola": "# Granola\n- Granola keeps its notes encrypted on this Mac and has no web app, so it is reached only through its own tools: the Granola MCP server (usually named \"granola\"), signed in either through Engelbart or through the person's own Claude Code or Codex, where they are signed in already. Its tools list or search meetings, get a meeting's notes and get its transcript. Use them; never ask the person to export or download anything.\n- Use only its tools that read (listing meetings and folders, getting a meeting's notes or transcript); never switch the workspace or sign out. List the meetings in the plan's time range; for each one the plan wants, get its notes and, if the plan asks for transcripts, its transcript; add_note one note per meeting: title = the meeting's title and date, markdown = the notes (then the transcript under a \"Transcript\" heading), source = \"granola:<meeting id>\".\n- Only if there are no Granola tools at all, or they say the sign-in expired, call needs_you with kind \"connector\" and reason \"Connect Granola\".\n- Leave out by default: 1:1s about hiring or personal matters, standups, and meetings the person said to skip.",
